{"id":1184,"date":"2020-11-23T15:06:38","date_gmt":"2020-11-23T23:06:38","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1184"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:26:26","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:26:26","slug":"codrone-arduino-void-functions","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-void-functions\/","title":{"rendered":"Void Functions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d58286e\" name=\"6a2215d58286e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a function?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Quick review of functions from the pairing lesson: they&#8217;re a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code into one named set. For example, if you want to connect to the last CoDrone you paired with, you can type in at least six lines of code, or you can just use the function <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.pair()<\/code>.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1185&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d5829d9\" name=\"6a2215d5829d9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There are two parts to\u00a0writing a function: the call and the declaration.<\/p>\n<p>You&#8217;ve already seen and used the call before &#8212; it&#8217;s just using the function! For example, if you wanted to use the function to connect to the previously paired CoDrone, you would use this for the call:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.pair()<\/pre>\n<p>Unless you&#8217;ve already written your own functions before, you probably haven&#8217;t used declarations yet. A declaration tells the program what you want the function to be able to do. This is what that usually looks like:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void functionName(inputs) {\r\n  statements\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is what&#8217;s going on here:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>We&#8217;ll be talking about this in a later lesson, but some functions will return a certain value. Nothing will be returning in this lesson, so you can write <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">void<\/code> instead.<\/li>\n<li><code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">functionName<\/code> is the name of the function.<\/li>\n<li>Inputs are what you want to receive. This could be a duration of time, the LEDs that should light up, the drone to connect to, or in some cases, like with <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">CoDrone.move()<\/code>, nothing at all.<\/li>\n<li>Statements are the code that will run when the function is called.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582a78\" name=\"6a2215d582a78\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Creating a function: GoForward()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Here&#8217;s an example of how to make a function.<\/p>\n<p>To make your CoDrone fly forward for 2 seconds, you would write:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\nCoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\nCoDrone.move(2);<\/pre>\n<p>That would work if you just want your CoDrone to go forward once. What happens if you&#8217;re doing a program where you want your CoDrone to look like it&#8217;s going up a flight of stairs? You could create a function called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">goForward()<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>\u00a0instead!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void goForward() {  \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Now, when you use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">void goForward()<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code>, your CoDrone will always pitch forward at 70 for 2 seconds.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {  \r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);  \r\n    CoDrone.pair();  \r\n    delay(200);  \/\/waits 200 milliseconds or 0.2 second\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(70);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2); \r\n \r\n    goForward();  \r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(60);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2); \r\n\r\n    goForward();\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(50);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2); \r\n\r\n    goForward();\r\n    \r\n\r\n    CoDrone.land();\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid goForward() {  \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Important for you to know: when you write a function, it should be outside of <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">void setup()<\/code><code class=\"code-snippet-inline\"><\/code> and <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">void loop()<\/code>. Unlike variables, functions do not need to be declared at the beginning of the program, so we put ours at the end.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\"><\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582b0f\" name=\"6a2215d582b0f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Declaring functions with inputs<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]One of the good things about functions is that they can accept inputs. For example, if you wanted to turn LED 11 on your Smart Inventor Board on, you could write this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(11,1)<\/pre>\n<p>Don&#8217;t forget, 1 is the same thing as HIGH, which is on! But if you changed your mind and wanted to turn LED 14 on instead, you would just write this:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(14,1);<\/pre>\n<p>The only thing that changed was the pin number. The function is the same because it&#8217;s performing (almost) the same operation.<\/p>\n<p>To declare, or write, a function that is able to do this, you would need to include variables that are only used within the function:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void myFunction(int x, int y) {\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Once\u00a0those\u00a0variables\u00a0are\u00a0declared,\u00a0you\u00a0can\u00a0use\u00a0them\u00a0inside\u00a0the\u00a0function!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void myFunction(int x, int y) {\r\n  Serial.print(x);\r\n  Serial.print(\"\u00a0\u00a0\");\r\n  Serial.println(y);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>If you want three (or more!) inputs, you can add them into the variable declaration.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void myFunction( int x, int y, int z, int namedWhateverYouLike) {\r\n}<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582bac\" name=\"6a2215d582bac\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Calling functions with inputs<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you call a function, make sure that you include the right number of values. You also need to make sure that you put your values in the right place. For example, if you created two variables in your function, you could use 5 and 9, and they would show up in that order. If you wanted to switch the order, you would have to write 9 and 5 in your program instead.<\/p>\n<p>Try this program out in Arduino&#8217;s serial monitor to see what we mean!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup() {  \r\n    Serial.begin(9600);\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() {  \r\n    MyFunction(5, 9);\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid MyFunction(int x, int y) {  \r\n    Serial.print(x);  \r\n    Serial.print(\"\u00a0\u00a0\");      \r\n    Serial.println(y);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Another example! What happens if you have the function below that has a delay of two seconds, but realize that sometimes, you might want your CoDrone to go forward to three seconds?<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void GoForward() {  \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>What you can do instead of going back and changing your milliseconds every. single. time. is create a variable that will store your time (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>).<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void GoForward(int duration){  \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\n    CoDrone.move(duration);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>When you call the function, you can use whatever time duration you want in the parentheses!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">GoForward(3);<\/pre>\n<p>Try this code on your own to see how it works.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt; \r\n\r\nvoid setup() {  \r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);  \r\n    CoDrone.pair();  \r\n    delay(200);    \/\/waits 200 milliseconds or 0.2 second\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n \r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(70);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);   \r\n\r\n    GoForward(3000);  \r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(60);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);  \r\n\r\n    GoForward(2000);\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(50);\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n\r\n    GoForward(1000);\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.land();\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() { \r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid GoForward(int duration) { \r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(70);\r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\n    CoDrone.move(duration);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582c45\" name=\"6a2215d582c45\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>LEDs made easy<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you wanted to turn your remote control&#8217;s LEDs on, you would have to use copy and paste a lot, or you&#8217;d have to be a really fast typer.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">digitalWrite(11, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(12, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(13, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(14, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(15, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(16, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(17, 1);\r\ndigitalWrite(18, 1);<\/pre>\n<p>To make your life easier, you can turn this into a function!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void LEDs(int p11, int p12, int p13, int p14, int p15, int p16, int p17, int p18) {\r\n  digitalWrite(11, p11);\r\n  digitalWrite(12, p11);\r\n  digitalWrite(13, p13);\r\n  digitalWrite(14, p14);\r\n  digitalWrite(15, p15);\r\n  digitalWrite(16, p16);\r\n  digitalWrite(17, p17);\r\n  digitalWrite(18, p18);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Now you can just write the function and a bunch of numbers. If you wanted all of your LEDs on at one time, you could write this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">LEDs( 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1);<\/pre>\n<p>If you wanted half of them off and half of them on, you could write this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">LEDs( 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0);<\/pre>\n<p>If you want to use high and low instead, you can do that too!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">LEDs(HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW, HIGH, LOW);<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582cd1\" name=\"6a2215d582cd1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight directions function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Another set of commands that you use a lot is flight commands! With this block of code, your CoDrone would fly up and forwards.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">CoDrone.setThrottle(60);\r\nCoDrone.setPitch(40);\r\nCoDrone.move();<\/pre>\n<p>To make this easier, you can create a function (we called ours <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Maneuver<\/code>) and include all of the flight directions as variables. We know this seems complicated with a lot of typing now, but we promise it will make everything easier!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void Maneuver (int throttle, int roll, int pitch, int yaw) {  \r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(throttle);\r\n    CoDrone.setRoll(roll);\r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(pitch);\r\n    CoDrone.setYaw(yaw); \r\n    CoDrone.move();\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>Once you include that, you can just use the numbers you need! If you wanted to use the same throttle and pitch from the example, it would look like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">Maneuver(60, 40, 0, 0);<\/pre>\n<p>This is what it would look like in a program. Try it out on your own to see what happens!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt; \r\n\r\nint StartFlag;\r\nunsigned long StartTime; \r\n\r\nvoid setup() {  \r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);  \r\n    CoDrone.pair();\r\n\r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n    CoDrone.move(2);\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() {  \r\n    byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);  \r\n    byte bt4 = digitalRead(14);  \r\n    byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);   \r\n\r\n    if (bt8) {    \r\n        StartFlag = 1;    \r\n        StartTime = millis();  \r\n    }   \r\n\r\n    if (StartFlag == 1) {    \r\n        if (millis() - StartTime &lt; 2000) {      \r\n            Maneuver(30, 0, 0, 0);    \r\n        }    \r\n        else if (millis() - StartTime &lt; 5000) {      \r\n            Maneuver(0, 0, 70, 0);    \r\n        }    \r\n        else {      \r\n            CoDrone.land();       \r\n            StartFlag = 0;    \r\n        }  \r\n    }   \r\n\r\n    if ( bt1) {    \r\n        CoDrone.emergencyStop();    \r\n        StartFlag = 0;  \r\n        }\r\n    } \r\n\r\n  void Maneuver (int throttle, int roll, int pitch, int yaw) {  \r\n    CoDrone.setThrottle(throttle);\r\n    CoDrone.setRoll(roll);\r\n    CoDrone.setPitch(pitch);\r\n    CoDrone.setYaw(yaw); \r\n    CoDrone.move();\r\n}<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d582d3e\" name=\"6a2215d582d3e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: Function Fiesta<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create CoDrone codes for three functions!<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>One of the functions should include the flight directions (you just learned how to do this!).<\/li>\n<li>Another function should control the CoDrone&#8217;s LEDs.<\/li>\n<li>The third function is your choice!<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 What is a function? [vc_column_text]Quick review of functions from the pairing lesson: they're a way to save time and energy by storing commonly used blocks of code&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-void-functions\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1749,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1815,1746,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2163,2164,2165,2131,2122,2201],"class_list":["post-1184","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-functions","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-void-functions","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1184","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1184\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1188,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1184\/revisions\/1188"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1749"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1184"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1184"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1184"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}